            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new run of words: what did anugrahaṁ mean, and what did vandanaṁ mean?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: വാതിൽ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "വാതിൽ (vātil) — \"a door\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tamil writes its door-word வாயில் (vāyil), and once you see it beside வாய் — the mouth this book has already taught — the picture is hard to unsee. A doorway is the mouth of a house."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Malayalam's വാതിൽ (vātil) is the same word with a t where Tamil has a y, and the dictionaries connect the two. The mouth-word itself, വായ്, sits in this book's chapter on the face."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada says ಬಾಗಿಲು (bāgilu), which is a different word altogether. The chillu ൽ at the end here is the same one that closed the leg-word and the finger-word."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The first of five things a guest meets at the door."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
