        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: കോലം",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "കോലം (kōlaṁ) — \"the pattern drawn at a doorway\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Native Dravidian, and its plain sense is 'form, shape, guise'. The pattern chalked on the ground at a threshold is a shape laid down, and Tamil uses கோலம் for the same thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The word keeps its wider life alongside the drawing: a person in costume, an animal's markings, anything that has taken on a form, can be a കോലം (kōlaṁ)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Kannada names the same threshold drawing ರಂಗೋಲಿ (raṅgōli), a word built on Sanskrit colour. One sister named the practice for its shape and the other for its pigment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three. A door, a chair, the pattern at the threshold."
            }
          ]
        },
